// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ⚠ NO COLUMN HERE CAN EVER HOLD A PLAINTEXT PROVIDER KEY
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `UserAiKey.secret` is ciphertext ONLY — the output of `encryptSecret(
// plaintext, 'ai_user_key')` (`@marinoscar/platform-api/core`), the same
// AES-GCM helper `Credential.secret` already uses, keyed under a purpose
// label specific to this table so a ciphertext lifted from one row cannot be
// decrypted as if it were the other's. `hint` is the non-secret display aid
// (`'••••' + last 4`, mirroring `Credential.hint`), never enough on its own
// to reconstruct the key. `AiRun.request` is explicitly a NORMALIZED request
// — model id, parameters, truncated/redacted prompt shape — never the
// caller's raw provider key, which the run's own executor already holds
// separately (from `UserAiKey`, decrypted for that one call and discarded).
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `UserAiKey` — Cascade, NOT SetNull, and the contrast with `Credential` is
// deliberate
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `Credential.updatedByUser` is `SetNull`: that table is shared
// infrastructure (an SMTP password, a storage secret) that outlives whoever
// last edited it, because the credential belongs to the DEPLOYMENT, not to
// the editor. `UserAiKey.user` is the opposite shape on purpose — a BYOK key
// belongs to the ONE ACCOUNT that typed it in, has no meaning to anyone else,
// and (like `PushSubscription.user`, which makes the identical argument for a
// live per-device credential) has no reader once that account is gone. There
// is no "the deployment's OpenAI key" here, only "this user's OpenAI key", so
// deleting the user is what should delete it.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `AiRun.user` / `AiUsageEvent.user` — SetNull, matching `NotificationDelivery
// .user`
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Both are HISTORY, not live state: a run's output and a usage event's token
// counts remain meaningful — for billing reconciliation, for "was this
// account affected by the incident on provider X" — after the account that
// triggered them is gone, exactly the argument `NotificationDelivery.user`'s
// own block comment makes for a delivery attempt. Nulling the FK preserves
// that record; cascading it would erase usage accounting as a side effect of
// an unrelated account-deletion path elsewhere in the app. Both are also
// deliberately nullable up front (not merely nullable-after-cascade), the same
// "global job" shape `Job.subjectType`/`subjectId` use: a background run or a
// catalog-discovery usage event may have no requesting user at all.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `capabilities` / `units` — JSONB, not typed columns, and Zod-validated in
// code
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `AiModel.capabilities` (what a model can do — text, vision, tool calling,
// structured output, ...) and `AiUsageEvent.units` (a per-operation unit
// count that varies by kind: `{ images: 2 }`, `{ audioSeconds: 31.4 }`) are
// each shaped differently per provider and per operation, and neither is
// filtered or indexed on by any query this issue's schema needs to support.
// Both follow `Job.payload`'s and `WorkerNode.capabilities`' own precedent:
// opaque JSONB here, a real Zod schema (`AiModelCapabilities`) at the
// consuming layer that arrives with #427/#428. A typed column per capability
// would be a migration every time a provider ships a new one.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `provider` / `operation` / `status` / `keySource` — plain strings, not
// Prisma enums
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Same reasoning as `Job.type`, `NotificationDelivery.channel` and
// `WorkerNode.executor` throughout this file: these are small, code-owned,
// GROWING sets (`AI_PROVIDER_IDS` names `'openai'` today and Phase 3 appends
// to it; new operations and key sources are exactly the shape of change a
// provider integration adds), so a Postgres enum would reintroduce the
// migration this design otherwise avoids, and a row must be able to outlive
// whatever build produced it. `AiRun.status` and `AiUsageEvent.status` are
// each that model's OWN closed state machine in every other sense — but they
// are kept as strings rather than promoted to a Prisma enum for the same
// forward-compatibility reason the rest of this section gives, not because
// they are open-ended in practice.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `AiModel.capabilitySource` — `'catalog' | 'admin_override' | 'unclassified'`
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Records WHERE `capabilities` came from, because the three sources disagree
// in trustworthiness and in how long they last: `'catalog'` is the
// provider's own advertised capability list (refreshed on discovery, and the
// first thing a later sync overwrites), `'admin_override'` is a deployment
// operator's manual correction (must survive a catalog refresh that would
// otherwise clobber it — that survival rule belongs to #427's sync logic, not
// to this column), and `'unclassified'` is the default for a model this
// deployment has seen but never classified at all. A model discovered before
// any classification runs is `'unclassified'`, never a guess.
model AiModel {
  id String @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid

  // The provider registration this model belongs to, e.g. `'openai'`. See
  // `AI_PROVIDER_IDS` (`common/schemas/settings.schema.ts`) — a growing,
  // code-owned list, and a plain string here for the reason given above.
  provider String

  // The provider's own raw model identifier (e.g. `'gpt-4o-mini'`), as
  // opposed to `id` above, which is this table's own row identity.
  modelId String @map("model_id")

  // Operator/UI-facing label. Nullable: a freshly discovered model has none
  // until an admin (or a later sync) names it.
  displayName String? @map("display_name")

  // AiModelCapabilities, Zod-validated at the consuming layer (#427) — see
  // the block comment above for why this is JSONB rather than typed columns.
  capabilities Json

  // Where `capabilities` came from — see the block comment above.
  // `'unclassified'` is the default: a model this deployment has observed but
  // not yet classified, one way or the other.
  capabilitySource String @default("unclassified") @map("capability_source")

  // Whether this deployment offers the model to callers at all. Defaults to
  // `false`: a freshly discovered model is inert until an administrator (or a
  // later sync's own policy) turns it on, the same "nothing changes behaviour
  // on discovery alone" posture `WorkerNode.status` defaulting to `offline`
  // and `databaseBackup.enabled` defaulting to `false` both take elsewhere in
  // this schema.
  enabled Boolean @default(false)

  contextWindow   Int? @map("context_window")
  maxOutputTokens Int? @map("max_output_tokens")

  // When this row was first created, vs. `lastSeenAt` below, which moves on
  // every discovery sync that still finds the model — the same "creation time
  // vs. liveness" split `WorkerNode.registeredAt`/`.lastHeartbeatAt` make for
  // an entirely different kind of inventory.
  discoveredAt DateTime  @default(now()) @map("discovered_at") @db.Timestamptz
  lastSeenAt   DateTime  @default(now()) @map("last_seen_at") @db.Timestamptz
  // Set by a discovery sync (#427) that no longer sees this model in the
  // provider's own catalog. Nullable: most rows are never deprecated.
  // Deliberately not deleted — a deprecated model may still be referenced by
  // historical `AiRun`/`AiUsageEvent` rows via its `provider`/`modelId` pair,
  // and losing the row would lose the display name and capability record for
  // that history.
  deprecatedAt DateTime? @map("deprecated_at") @db.Timestamptz

  // Provenance of the last edit — see the block comment above for why this
  // is `SetNull`, matching `Credential.updatedByUser` rather than
  // `UserAiKey.user` below.
